Facts: {"actor": null, "malware": null, "cves": ["CVE-2019-19781"], "ttps": null, "targets": null, "sectors": null, "indicators_types": null, "timeline": ["January 19, 2020: Citrix released firmware updates for Citrix Application Delivery Controller (ADC) and Citrix Gateway versions 11.1 and 12.0.", "January 22, 2020: Citrix released security updates for vulnerable SD-WAN WANOP appliances.", "January 23, 2020: Citrix released firmware updates for Citrix ADC and Gateway versions 12.1 and 13.0.", "January 24, 2020: Citrix released firmware updates for Citrix ADC and Gateway version 10.5.", "December 17, 2019: Citrix reported vulnerability CVE-2019-19781."], "impact": "A remote, unauthenticated attacker could exploit CVE-2019-19781 to perform arbitrary code execution.", "mitigations": ["Apply Citrix’s interim mitigation steps for CVE-2019-19781.", "Consider deploying a VPN capability using standardized protocols, preferably ones listed on the National Information Assurance Partnership (NIAP) Product Compliant List (PCL), in front of publicly accessible Citrix ADC and Citrix Gateway appliances to require user authentication for the VPN before being able to reach these appliances."]}

Draft:
This internal security bulletin serves to highlight the critical risks associated with CVE-2019-19781, a severe vulnerability first reported by Citrix on December 17, 2019. This security flaw is particularly dangerous because it allows a remote, unauthenticated attacker to bypass standard security controls and perform arbitrary code execution on affected systems. Given the potential for total system compromise, immediate attention to our infrastructure is required. Following the initial disclosure, Citrix initiated a comprehensive rollout of firmware updates to address the underlying issues across their product lines. The remediation timeline began in earnest on January 19, 2020, with the release of firmware updates for Citrix Application Delivery Controller (ADC) and Citrix Gateway versions 11.1 and 12.0. This was followed shortly by security updates for vulnerable SD-WAN WANOP appliances on January 22, 2020. Citrix continued their patch management efforts on January 23, 2020, by releasing firmware updates for versions 12.1 and 13.0, and concluded the primary cycle on January 24, 2020, with updates for Citrix ADC and Gateway version 10.5. To ensure our defensive posture remains robust, all relevant systems must be audited against these versions immediately. Beyond applying the necessary vendor patches, it is imperative that we implement the interim mitigation steps provided by Citrix to reduce the attack surface. Furthermore, as a proactive architectural defense, we should consider deploying VPN capabilities using standardized protocols in front of all publicly accessible Citrix ADC and Gateway appliances. Ideally, these VPN solutions should be selected from the National Information Assurance Partnership (NIAP) Product Compliant List (PCL). By enforcing user authentication at the VPN layer before traffic can reach the Citrix appliances, we can effectively mitigate the threat of unauthenticated remote exploitation. SOC analysts should prioritize monitoring for any unauthorized activity targeting these specific gateways and report any anomalies immediately to the incident response team. Maintaining rigorous patch hygiene and network segmentation remains our best strategy for neutralizing the risk posed by this vulnerability.

