Government agencies and private sector organizations should be advised of ongoing exploitation efforts targeting critical vulnerabilities in VMware products. Malicious cyber actors, assessed to be advanced persistent threat (APT) groups, are actively leveraging a chain of vulnerabilities to compromise enterprise environments. The security flaws, specifically identified as CVE-2022-22954, CVE-2022-22960, CVE-2022-22972, and CVE-2022-22973, present a severe risk to network integrity. When successfully exploited, these vulnerabilities allow unauthorized actors to trigger server-side template injection, which can facilitate remote code execution or facilitate the escalation of privileges to the root level. VMware issued comprehensive security updates to address these weaknesses on April 6, 2022; however, subsequent analysis confirmed that these flaws were being weaponized against unpatched systems in the wild. Consequently, the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) added CVE-2022-22954 and CVE-2022-22960 to its catalog of Known Exploited Vulnerabilities on April 14 and April 15, 2022, respectively. To ensure the security of federal and critical infrastructure systems, binding directives necessitated that federal agencies complete the application of these security updates by early May 2022. Despite these mandates, the persistence of these vulnerabilities in legacy or unmanaged systems continues to provide opportunities for adversary lateral movement and long-term persistence within target networks. Network defenders are urged to prioritize the immediate application of all available patches provided by the vendor. In environments where immediate patching is operationally unfeasible, administrators must treat the affected software as compromised and remove the instances from the network perimeter until the requisite updates can be fully verified and deployed. Organizations are encouraged to review internal logs for anomalous activity consistent with privilege escalation and remote command execution, as these indicators serve as primary evidence of exploitation attempts. By maintaining a rigorous patching cadence and ensuring that all exposed infrastructure is hardened against these specific vulnerabilities, organizations can significantly reduce the risk of successful exploitation by APT actors aiming to gain unauthorized administrative access to enterprise resources and sensitive data repositories.

